“Modi Ka Baap Bhi Jayega”: Congress leader Mallikarjun Kharge sparks fury by insulting PM Modi
Mallikarjun Kharge’s “Modi ka baap bhi jayega” remark in Patna has triggered a political row, renewing scrutiny of the Congress’s personal attacks on Prime Minister Narendra Modi. His call to dismantle RSS ideology has also drawn criticism, with questions over the Congress president’s stance on ideological pluralism and the organisation’s century-long history
WEBDESK
Oct 26, 2026, 07:45 am IST
“Modi Ka Baap Bhi Jayega”: Congress leader Mallikarjun Kharge sparks fury by insulting PM Modi
Kharge’s Modi Remark and Attack on RSS Spark Row


NEW DELHI: Congress President Mallikarjun Kharge has faced sharp criticism after he made a controversial remark about Prime Minister Narendra Modi at a public event in Patna on October 26. The comment triggered a political controversy, with BJP leaders and social media users questioning the language used by a senior opposition leader against the country’s elected Prime Minister.

The controversy erupted during an event marking the 139th birth anniversary of former Bihar Chief Minister Dr Shri Krishna Singh. While Kharge was addressing the gathering, a person in the audience reportedly interrupted him, saying that Modi would not leave office. Responding to the remark, Kharge said, “Modi ka baap bhi jayega,” a statement that was widely circulated in video clips on social media.

कांग्रेस की राजनीति का स्तर आज कहाँ पहुँच गया है, यह उसके शीर्ष नेतृत्व की भाषा और व्यवहार से साफ दिखाई देता है।

जिस भाषा को कभी सड़कछाप राजनीति और दबंगई से जोड़ा जाता था, आज उसी भाषा का इस्तेमाल कांग्रेस अध्यक्ष कर रहे हैं। कांग्रेस की राजनीति में शालीनता, संयम और लोकतांत्रिक… pic.twitter.com/be5Ierxg3V

— Sambit Patra (@sambitswaraj) October 25, 2026

The remark quickly became a subject of political debate, with critics arguing that the Congress president should have exercised greater restraint while responding to an audience member. Social media discussions focused on whether such language was appropriate for a leader holding the highest organisational position in a national political party.

Also Read: Jammu & Kashmir Accession Day: When RSS and Guruji Golwalkar stood for unity and integrity of Bharat
Congress and its history of abusing PM Modi
The Congress’s hostility towards Prime Minister Narendra Modi has repeatedly spilled over from political criticism into personal attacks, with party leaders using labels such as Sonia Gandhi’s “maut ka saudagar” in 2007, Mani Shankar Aiyar’s “neech aadmi” remark in 2017 and Mallikarjun Kharge’s comparison of Modi to “Ravan” in 2022.

Rahul Gandhi, too, has made Modi the target of sharp personal and political attacks, including the “Chowkidar chor hai” slogan during the 2019 election campaign.

Now, Kharge’s October 25, 2026, retort in Patna,  “Modi ka baap bhi jayega” after an audience member insisted that the Prime Minister would not leave office has triggered another controversy.

Political disagreement is an essential part of democracy, but repeatedly resorting to personal jibes raises questions about the Congress’s political messaging and its ability to challenge Modi on policies and governance without making the Prime Minister himself the centre of its attacks.

Kharge abused RSS, an organisation serving Bharat for past 100 years now
Kharge said that India can have no lasting peace until RSS ideology is dismantled is a sweeping political attack that treats an entire organisation’s worldview as a threat without establishing how that claim follows from evidence. Founded in 1925, the RSS has completed 100 years of service, with its swayamsevaks involved in social service, relief work and community initiatives across the country.

Disagreement with its ideology is legitimate in a democracy, but demanding its ideological elimination raises a basic question: does democratic pluralism mean allowing competing ideas to exist, or seeking to erase those one opposes?

Kharge’s criticism of Nitish Kumar for joining the BJP-led alliance further underlines that his speech was also a political attack on rival parties and their alliances.

The larger issue is that Kharge declared that peace depends on eliminating an opposing ideology. A democracy cannot be strengthened by treating ideological opponents as people whose beliefs must be uprooted.

The RSS is be open to scrutiny and criticism, like every organisation, but those criticisms should rest on verifiable facts rather than sweeping accusations. After a century of service to the nation, the organisation’s ideas and public record can be debated on their merits; calling for their elimination does not settle that debate; it intensifies political polarisation instead.


